\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}

\title{What the route answers}

\p{The note suite can now return to the questions that opened it. The
answers are deliberately scoped: each conclusion names its state model,
its evidence, and the work that remains outside the chapter.}

\ol{
  \li{\strong{What does a local turn carry?} [Incidence, symmetry and
  moves](frap-0002) separates shape incidence from the local action, while
  [positions and orientations](frap-000A) records the transported frame.
  [How the cube models connect](frap-001A) then maps the 54 fixed-frame
  facet positions to corner and edge fibres, six fixed centers, and the
  ordinary #{(\sigma,x;\tau,z)} coordinates. The map also states what
  changes for #{N>3} and what is forgotten by a colour quotient.}

  \li{\strong{Why do familiar cube constraints appear?} [Transfer and
  dimension](frap-000B) explains the orientation character and its limits;
  the [complete three-layer cube](frap-0016) checks the equal permutation
  signs and the two orientation sums directly on the six outer generators,
  then gives a constructive cleanup for every state satisfying them.}

  \li{\strong{How can a person solve?} [Human methods](frap-0005) names
  recognition, preservation, memory, and move cost as separate concerns.
  The [complete layer-first method](frap-0017) supplies a seven-stage
  construction with stabilizers, checked triggers, and termination reasons.
  It is a completeness argument for the declared model, not an optimality
  claim.}

  \li{\strong{What can a computer certify?} [Computer solving](frap-0018)
  separates an upper-bound word, an admissible projected lower bound, and an
  exhaustive shortest-path certificate. The [worked return to play](frap-001B)
  instantiates that distinction on one fixed-frame scramble: inverse replay,
  #{3\le d_{\rm HTM}\le10}, and no claim that ten is
  shortest.}

  \li{\strong{What do the outlooks contribute?} [Label transport and
  topology](frap-0014) identifies the fibre data needed before a branched
  covering analogy can be tested. [Scrambling, spectra and search
  coordinates](frap-0015) states the finite random-walk and representation
  questions without turning them into a solver claim. [Which moves survive a
  quotient?](frap-0019) gives the exact descent condition for an observation
  quotient to inherit the move action. These are connected applications of
  the move-and-frame language, with their own hypotheses and constructions.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{Status and next boundary}

  \p{The route therefore closes as a readable mathematical specification:
  abstract incidence leads to framed moves, framed moves lead to the
  all-size and three-layer laws, and those laws return to a human method, a
  computer certificate, and one replayed scramble. The examples preserve the
  distinction between a valid solve, a complete method, a short word, and a
  proved optimum.}

  \p{The remaining boundary is explicit. These notes do not yet contain a
  Lean-certified puzzle model, a verified executable solver, or a general
  comparison of human methods by measured solving data. Those are later
  programmes: they may reuse the state maps, invariants, and replay contract
  established here, but they require separate formal and experimental
  evidence.}
}
